atus, and those arguments remain influential. Politics and history help explain why particular rules are enforced, by whom, and with what intensity; they do not make the rules harmless or erase their religious basis. At the same time, the people challenging these systems are often Muslims themselves—women’s-rights campaigners, lawyers, writers, secularists, reformist believers, and ex-Muslims. Supporting their freedom to criticize, reform, leave, or reject religion is more useful than treating all Muslims as interchangeable with the authorities enforcing oppressive laws.
